{
  "measured_on": "github-actions ubuntu-latest, test (3.11), -n auto",
  "note": "UX-420: one CI run's per-file totals, so a later CI run can be read against CI rather than against the floors in tests/tiers.py, which describe a developer machine. Refresh with tools/dev_tier_drift.py --record. UX-427: two files added in round 67 were absent and were appended from run 33318288027 (test (3.11), head 2527114) rather than by re-recording the whole document - that run's own spread reached x28.4, so its numbers are not a baseline for the other 369 files. UX-432: this round's new file was appended the same way, from run 33325461816 (test (3.11), head 26f5db9) at 6.12s - that run's spread reached x5.87 against a shift of x1.23, healthy enough to read one file's number from but still not a re-record of the other 370. UX-441/442 round 70: two files this round added were absent and were appended the same way, from run 33362420800 (test (3.11), head 3ccdeea) - 8.4s and 1.7s as the gate printed them, divided by that run's x1.28 shift to put them on this document's own clock, giving 6.56 and 1.33. Divided rather than appended raw, which is where this departs from the UX-427 and UX-432 appends above: `expected = known * shift` is the arithmetic the gate does, so a raw append embeds that run's shift as permanent leniency for those rows (23% in UX-432's case). The candidate artifact UX-441 uploads is the better source and was unreachable - this session's egress proxy refuses the artifact blob store - so these two come from the gate's own printed line, at its one decimal place. `UX-429`'s file was appended the same way from run 33366717100 (test (3.11), head 1244e6b) - 3.0s over that run's x1.28 shift, giving 2.34. Third append this round: every new test file over the medium floor costs one CI round-trip to measure, which `UX-447` should say out loud when it documents the refresh route. `UX-436`'s file followed from run 33369460064 (test (3.11), head 2d4ed13) - 9.0s over that run's x1.33 shift, giving 6.77 against 6.7s measured on a developer machine. The two agreeing to within 1% is the evidence for dividing rather than appending raw: the raw 9.0 would have been 34% of permanent slack on that row. `UX-435`'s file was the fifth and last of the round, from run 33373772012 (test (3.11), head 1764d7c) - 15.0s over that run's x1.29 shift, giving 11.63 against 14.2s measured on a developer machine. That run was 29% slower than this document's clock, so the raw 15.0 would have carried 29% of permanent slack on the row. Five appends in one round is the measurement `UX-447` needs: the refresh route costs one CI round-trip per new file over the medium floor. `UX-437`'s census file is not here and needs no row - 0.4s, below `tests/tiers.py`'s MEDIUM_FLOOR_S, so the gate does not report it. Round 71, `UX-449`: 13.7s over that run's x1.23 shift (run 33385852074, test (3.11), head 2456465), giving 11.14 - against 3.2s measured on a developer machine. That 4.3x gap is much wider than the shift and is the file's own shape rather than the runner's: it parses every test source in the suite (~380 files, 195 skip call sites), so it is I/O-bound where the rest of the suite is not, and CI's disk is where that shows. Worth knowing before another whole-tree parser is added. Round 71, `UX-451`: this document's row for `test_the_handoff_box_is_measured_served.py` was **scaled**, not re-measured on CI - 11.63 x (28.78/14.22) = 23.54. The file gained eight clauses that drive a second served page and a second browser, and the same developer machine that produced the 14.22s baseline produced 28.78s afterwards. Scaling by a file's own before/after ratio on one machine keeps the shift normalisation the append recipe above is careful about - the runner cancels - where re-measuring would cost the CI round-trip that recipe already says every new file over the medium floor costs. It is still a ratio and not a CI reading: if the drift gate disagrees on the next run, the gate's printed line over that run's shift is the better number and should replace this.",
